<a href="#section-send-architecture" id="section-send-architecture" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
<h2>Inside a mail send</h2>
<section>
  <p class="wide">These are the pieces behind the configuration on this page. The executor controls waiting work, the pool manages reusable
    connections, and the transport talks SMTP. Outcome reporting follows each email across those layers; it is separate from the work of sending it.</p>
  {{> components/mail-send-architecture}}
  <p class="wide">This is a responsibility map, not a lock-order diagram. Direct sends, shared batches, custom mailers, and caller-owned Sessions
    have different paths. Use the sections below for their execution and ownership rules.</p>
</section>

<a href="#section-send-execution" id="section-send-execution" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
<h2>Choose how sends execute</h2>
<section>
  <div class="view">
    <p><code>mailer.sync().sendMail(email)</code> finishes on the calling thread and throws failures directly.
      It returns a submission receipt; ignore it when only completion matters.
      <code>mailer.async().sendMail(email)</code> returns <code>MailSend&lt;MailSubmissionReceipt&gt;</code> for background SMTP work.</p>
    <p>Both views are cached and share one Mailer, its resources and its lifecycle. Obtaining a view starts no work.
      Choose the mode per operation: there is no builder async default or boolean execution-mode argument.
      <code>testConnection()</code> and <code>probeConnection()</code> use the same sync/async views.</p>
    <p><code>getCompletion()</code> returns a detached CompletableFuture for the actual result. Each call creates a fresh view.
      Completing, cancelling, or applying <code>orTimeout(...)</code> to that view cannot change the send.
      Use <code>requestCancellation()</code> when you intend to stop the underlying work.</p>
    <p>Async sends select their destination, apply its Email templates and validate during executor execution. Those failures go onto the returned
      completion; a null email remains an immediate argument error. Direct or caller-runs executors may execute on the caller itself.
      Simple batches retain lazy iteration on their execution thread.</p>
    <p>Ordinary <code>buildEmail()</code> creates the submitted message. The selected destination combines its applicable defaults and overrides
      during preparation, using each field's documented precedence or merge rules. Use
      <code>buildEmailCompletedWithDefaultsAndOverrides(...)</code> when you explicitly need that completion earlier; see
      <a href="understanding-configuration.html#configuration-resolution-timing">when choices are combined</a>.</p>
    <p>To use another permitted server after a connection failure, configure <a href="/smtp-connection-failover.html">SMTP connection failover</a>.
      It shares this operation's queue, deadline, cancellation and observer. Busy pools and sending-limit waits do not trigger it.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="side"><pre><code class="language-java">MailSend&lt;MailSubmissionReceipt&gt; send =
    mailer.async().sendMail(email);

send.getCompletion().whenComplete((receipt, failure) -&gt; {
    if (failure == null) {
        recordSubmission(receipt);
    } else {
        recordFailure(failure);
    }
});

// Or wait, without losing the ability to request cancellation:
MailSubmissionReceipt receipt = send.getCompletion().join();</code></pre></div>
</section>
  <a href="#section-async-queue" id="section-async-queue" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  {{#> components/feature-heading-h2 core=true}}Bound asynchronous work and handle a full queue{{/components/feature-heading-h2}}

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        A background job can produce emails faster than the SMTP server accepts them. Limiting workers alone leaves the waiting queue free to grow.
        Set an async queue capacity when you want the application to notice overload and decide whether to pause the producer, retain work in its own
        durable queue, or skip a notification that is no longer useful.
      </p>
      <p>
        The default capacity is <code>-1</code> (unbounded). A positive capacity limits waiting tasks; <code>0</code> permits only immediate handoff
        to a worker. This is one queue per Mailer, not per SMTP connection or cluster. The SMTP work still runs on a worker, never as a full-queue fallback on the caller.
      </p>
      <p>
        The example rejects immediately once workers and waiting slots are occupied. Rejection completes the returned future exceptionally with
        <code>MailSendRejectedException</code>. An ordinary email attempt also reaches the configured observer once on the submitting thread, with no
        submission receipt and no started timestamp. No transport or proxy is acquired for the rejected task.
        This exception extends <code>MailException</code>, with kind <code>CAPACITY</code>; a generic library-failure handler can catch it too.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-java small">Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer("smtp.example.org", 587, "user", "password")
    .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP_TLS)
    .withAsyncExecutionConfig(AsyncExecutionConfig.builder()
        .withThreadPoolSize(4) // explicit worker count also applies without the batch module
        .withQueueCapacity(100)
        .rejectWhenFull()
        .build())
    .buildMailer();

mailer.async().sendMail(email).getCompletion().whenComplete((ignored, failure) -&gt; {
    Throwable cause = failure instanceof CompletionException ? failure.getCause() : failure;
    if (cause instanceof MailSendRejectedException) {
        MailSendRejectedException rejected = (MailSendRejectedException) cause;
        logger.warn("Mail was not scheduled: {}", rejected.getReason());
    } else if (cause != null) {
        logger.warn("Mail send failed", cause);
    }
});</code></pre>
      <p>
        Rejection means this attempt was not scheduled. Other send failures can represent an accepted or ambiguous SMTP transaction:
        inspect the <a href="features.html#section-submission-receipts">submission receipt</a> before deciding to retry.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <h4>Let a background producer wait briefly</h4>
      <p>
        For a producer that can slow down, <code>waitForCapacity(Duration)</code> waits on the submitting thread until a queue slot opens or the positive
        admission timeout expires. Pass that limit in the same call. Avoid waiting on request or event-loop threads that must return promptly.
        An observer submitting another async send can also wait; with a full one-worker queue, only the timeout may let that worker continue.
      </p>
      <p>
        <code>CAPACITY_WAIT_TIMED_OUT</code>, <code>CAPACITY_WAIT_INTERRUPTED</code>, and <code>EXECUTOR_SHUT_DOWN</code> explain unsuccessful waits;
        <code>QUEUE_FULL</code> is immediate rejection. An interrupted admission wait preserves the calling thread's interrupt flag.
        A borrowed executor's <code>RejectedExecutionException</code> becomes <code>MailSendRejectedException</code> with reason
        <code>EXECUTOR_REJECTED</code> and the original rejection as its cause; it does not invent a built-in queue count.
        The wait timeout is not a send deadline and does not cancel SMTP.
      </p>
<pre><code class="language-java small">mailerBuilder.withAsyncExecutionConfig(AsyncExecutionConfig.builder()
    .withQueueCapacity(100)
    .waitForCapacity(Duration.ofMillis(250))
    .build());</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="side">
      <h4>Know what the bound counts</h4>
      <p>
        Worker count, waiting queue capacity, and connection-pool size are independent. A worker waiting for a pool lease still counts as active.
        Queued tasks are FIFO; concurrent execution and completion need not be. Destination-specific Email preparation runs during execution,
        so it uses a worker slot too. The bound does not limit memory your application already used to construct the Email or its attachments.
      </p>
      <p>
        An asynchronous simple batch takes one slot and opens its iterable only when execution starts. Rejected batches do not touch the iterator or
        report outcomes for its emails. Async connection tests share the queue; synchronous and open-connection operations do not use it.
      </p>
      <p>
        A caller-owned executor defines its own queue, policy, and shutdown. Supply it through
        <code>withAsyncExecutionConfig(AsyncExecutionConfig.builder().withExecutorService(executor).build())</code>.
        Replacing the group removes previous explicit built-in settings. Do not combine the custom executor with built-in queue customization.
        The standalone <code>BatchTransportExecutor</code> has a separate executor and is not configured by these settings.
      </p>
      <p>
        Use <a href="debugging.html#section-async-queue">queue snapshots</a> for counts and rejection reasons, and
        <a href="/analyzing-send-results.html#section-send-timings">actual-send measurements</a> for individual scheduling delay. For lifecycle behavior, see
        <a href="#section-mailer-lifecycle">graceful shutdown</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>


<a href="#section-sending-limits" id="section-sending-limits" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
{{#> components/feature-heading-h2 core=true}}Match your SMTP service's sending limits{{/components/feature-heading-h2}}
{{> components/pattern-examples topic="sending-limits"}}
<section>
  <div class="view">
    <p>Your SMTP service allows a certain number of messages or recipients per minute, but several workers are sending at once.
      Configure those limits on the Mailer instead of putting sleeps around each send. They apply to synchronous and asynchronous sends,
      simple batches, and <code>withOpenConnection(...)</code>.</p>
    <p>Sending limits are disabled by default. Choose the count and period to match your service; <strong>30 per minute is just an example</strong>.
      If you configure both message and recipient limits, both must allow the next email. These are rolling windows, not counters that reset
      at the start of each clock minute.</p>
    <p>Connection-pool size still controls concurrent connections, and queue capacity still controls waiting tasks. Neither is a sending rate.
      These limits do not regulate bandwidth or coordinate your service account across applications.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-java small">SimpleJavaMail mail = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults();

Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer("smtp.example.org", 587, "user", "password")
    .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP_TLS)
    .withSendingLimitsConfig(SendingLimitsConfig.builder()
        .withMessageRateLimit(30, Duration.ofMinutes(1))
        .withRecipientRateLimit(100, Duration.ofMinutes(1))
        .withRateLimitGroup("company-account")
        .withRateLimitBurstsAllowed(false)
        .build())
    .buildMailer();

// The same send API; the caller or worker waits when needed.
mailer.async().sendMail(email);</code></pre>
    <p>On <code>SendingLimitsConfig.Builder</code>, calling a setter again replaces that rule. Use <code>resetMessageRateLimit()</code> or <code>resetRecipientRateLimit()</code>
      to remove it. <code>resetRateLimitGroup()</code> restores private allowance, while <code>resetRateLimitBurstsAllowed()</code>
      restores the default of allowing bursts. Supply the resulting value through <code>withSendingLimitsConfig(...)</code>.
      These resets affect the next Mailer you build, not an existing Mailer.</p>
  </div>
</section>
<section>
  <div class="view">
    <h3>Allow a burst, or spread sends out</h3>
    <p>Bursts are allowed by default: a 30-per-minute limit can submit 30 messages immediately, then wait for earlier attempts to leave
      the rolling window. Set <code>withRateLimitBurstsAllowed(false)</code> to spread starts out as well.</p>
    <p>With spreading, 30 messages per minute means at least two seconds between local submission starts; 120 per minute means half a second.
      The first can start immediately. A slow submission does not add another full interval after it finishes, and submissions may overlap.
      Idle time does not build up catch-up credit.</p>
    <p>Recipients have a weight: sending to ten recipients against a 100-per-minute rule puts at least six seconds before the following
      submission starts. The rolling-window ceiling still applies. An email with more recipients than the whole configured allowance is
      rejected clearly, not split up or left waiting forever.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="side">
    <h3>Use properties when the deployment chooses the rate</h3>
<pre><code class="language-properties small">simplejavamail.smtp.ratelimit.group=company-account
simplejavamail.smtp.ratelimit.messages.limit=30
simplejavamail.smtp.ratelimit.messages.period=PT1M
simplejavamail.smtp.ratelimit.recipients.limit=100
simplejavamail.smtp.ratelimit.recipients.period=PT1M
simplejavamail.smtp.ratelimit.allowbursts=false</code></pre>
    <p>Each rule needs a positive count and a positive ISO-8601 duration, such as <code>PT1M</code> or <code>PT0.5S</code>.
      Remove both the count and period to leave that rule disabled. See <a href="configuration.html#section-config-properties">configuration precedence</a>,
      <a href="spring.html#section-spring-sending-limits">Spring configuration</a>, and <a href="cli.html#section-sending-limits">CLI usage</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>
<section>
  <div class="view">
    <h3>Share a service account's allowance deliberately</h3>
    <p>Keep one <code>SimpleJavaMail</code> factory in application scope. Mailers built from that factory with the same rate-limit group share
      one allowance, even if they use different connections or pools. Their message rule, recipient rule, and burst setting must agree;
      conflicting declarations fail when building the Mailer.</p>
    <p>Without a group name, each registered SMTP configuration has its own allowance. A group is not a pool cluster or hostname.
      In a connection-pool cluster, the <em>selected SMTP configuration's</em> limits apply, which may differ from those of the Mailer you called.</p>
    <p>Closing and rebuilding a participant does not reset its named group's recent history. A failed build does not establish new group rules.
      Another factory, JVM, container, or CLI process has independent history, even with identical properties and group names.
      Keep any daily, persistent, or account-wide quota outside this in-process limiter.</p>
    <p>Reusing a caller-owned Session does not transfer one Mailer's limits to another. Within one pool cluster, however, the same Session identifies
      one pool: reuse that Mailer or its factory's rate-limit group, or use a separate Session for a separately configured participant.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="side">
    <h3>Know what counts</h3>
    <p>Allowance is reserved before ordinary transport acquisition or proxy startup, then charged when Simple Java Mail invokes the provider.
      Cancellation, connection failure, or MIME-preparation failure before invocation releases the unused reservation.
      Once invoked, the attempt stays charged, including rejection, uncertain acceptance, provider-side preflight failure, or later cleanup failure.
      This is attempted submission, not a count of accepted or delivered mail.</p>
    <p>Recipient cost follows the intended SMTP envelope, including BCC, additional recipient headers, and envelope overrides.
      Repeated addresses count as repeated occurrences. Rejected recipients are not refunded.
      <a href="features.html#section-custom-mailer">CustomMailer</a> counts one callback invocation and the intended envelope; retries or extra sends
      inside that callback are outside the limit. Validation, rehearsal, connection tests, probing, and logging-only mode consume nothing.</p>
    <p>Waiting uses the existing caller or worker thread. It responds to cancellation and configured send deadlines, but there is no separate
      rate-wait timeout. Live waiters are served FIFO, so a large email can hold up smaller ones. Bound the async queue if your producer can
      outrun this rate; adding workers does not increase a shared allowance.</p>
  </div>
</section>
<section>
  <p class="wide">Ordinary sends wait without borrowing a connection. Simple batches and <code>withOpenConnection(...)</code> deliberately keep
    their shared connection open during per-email waits. A long wait can exceed the server's idle timeout; Simple Java Mail does not reconnect
    or bypass your limit automatically. Prefer ordinary pooled sends when waits are likely to be long. Graceful shutdown also waits for accepted
    rate-limited work; retain async handles or configure a deadline if you need to bound that wait.</p>
  <p class="wide">Read the resolved rules through <code>mailer.getOperationalConfig()</code>, trace their sources through
    <a href="debugging.html#section-config-diagnostics">configuration diagnostics</a>, and inspect deliberate waiting with
    <a href="analyzing-send-results.html#section-send-timings"><code>MailSendDiagnostics.getRateLimitWait()</code></a>.
    The rate is based on local provider invocations, not guaranteed arrival times at the server.</p>
</section>

<a href="#section-send-deadlines" id="section-send-deadlines" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
<h2>Deadlines and cancellation</h2>
<section>
  <div class="view">
    <p>A notification may stop being useful while it waits: a campaign was paused, a job was cancelled, or a newer status update replaced it.
      Keep its send handle if you want to request cancellation. For a whole-operation time budget, configure a Mailer deadline.</p>
    <p><code>requestCancellation()</code> is a void, idempotent request, not an acknowledgement. Always inspect the eventual completion.
      A send can still succeed if SMTP acceptance was already observed. If the final reply was lost after the terminating DATA command
      or BDAT LAST could have reached the server, the receipt remains UNKNOWN with DUPLICATE_RISK. Cancellation does not recall accepted mail.</p>
    <p><code>MailSendCancelledException</code> and <code>MailSendTimeoutException</code> describe execution control, not new SMTP statuses.
      Both can contain an optional submission receipt. Before preparation/scheduling or while waiting for a connection, there may be no receipt.
      <a href="/analyzing-send-results.html#section-control-results">Read those receipts before deciding to retry.</a></p>
  </div>
  <div class="side"><pre><code class="language-java">Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withMailSendTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(30))
    .buildMailer();

MailSend&lt;MailSubmissionReceipt&gt; send = mailer.async().sendMail(email);

// Called by application code when this notification is obsolete:
send.requestCancellation();

// It resolves after execution exits and required cleanup finishes,
// not merely because cancellation was requested.
send.getCompletion().whenComplete(this::recordCompletion);</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-properties"># Optional; omitted means no total send deadline.
simplejavamail.defaults.mailsend.timeout=PT30S</code></pre>
<p>The same property works in <a href="/spring.html">Spring</a>. The CLI builder option takes ISO-8601 text too:
  <code>--mailer:withMailSendTimeout PT30S</code>. <code>resetMailSendTimeout()</code> removes a configured total deadline.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="wide">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Limit</th><th>What it bounds</th><th>What it does not mean</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>ConnectionConfig.Builder.withSessionTimeout(...)</code>, or individual <code>withConnectTimeout(...)</code>,
          <code>withReadTimeout(...)</code> and <code>withWriteTimeout(...)</code></td><td>Each socket operation's connect/read/write wait.
          The common setter selects all three.</td><td>Not a budget for the whole send. See <a href="configuration.html#socket-timeouts">fine-tuning and precedence</a>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>AsyncExecutionConfig.Builder.waitForCapacity(...)</code></td><td>Caller-side admission wait.</td><td>Does not cancel already admitted SMTP work.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>ConnectionPoolConfig.Builder.withClaimTimeout(...)</code></td><td>Waiting to obtain a pooled transport.</td><td>Not a deadline for using that transport.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>withMailSendTimeout(...)</code></td><td>One budget across preparation, admission, pool acquisition, connection/TLS/authentication,
          MIME/security work, SMTP, and required cleanup.</td><td>Not proof of non-acceptance, and not an unconditional return-by time.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>The total timeout is disabled by default and must be a positive, nanosecond-representable Duration. Existing shorter socket/acquisition limits
      still apply. A simple batch has one budget for its whole iteration, excluding observer work between emails.
      An open-connection operation gives connection establishment and each sender invocation separate budgets;
      arbitrary application work between sends and final scope cleanup are not charged to a send.</p>
    <p>Write-timeout scheduling on an owned managed Angus Session uses one lazy daemon worker shared by its physical connections.
      Returning a healthy connection to the pool or aborting its raw socket does not release that connection's scheduler reference;
      complete disposal does. The last disposal stops the worker, and a later connection can start a new one.
      Application-supplied timeout schedulers are not shut down by the Mailer. See <a href="configuration.html#section-programmatic-api-other">configuration options</a>.</p>
    <p>Supported SJM-owned Angus Sessions capture the socket before connection establishment, including TLS and supported proxy paths.
      Cancellation closes that socket without waiting for Angus's SMTP monitor. A pooled abort invalidates its lease and waits for disposal;
      a retired request cannot reach a later borrower. Queued cancellation removes owned queue work and releases its waiting slot.
      With a caller executor, a retired wrapper cannot send even if the executor cannot remove it.</p>
    <p>A configured total deadline fails before connecting if a selected provider, custom socket factory, CustomMailer, or caller-owned Session
      does not expose supported physical-abort capability. SJM does not silently replace caller-owned Session providers or socket factories.
      Without a total deadline, those integrations keep working, with cancellation at cooperative boundaries only.</p>
    <p>A shared pool cluster used for timed sends needs that capability on every member, because the pool may select any member.
      Once a timed send uses a cluster, newly registered members are checked too, until that cluster has no registered Sessions left.</p>
    <p>DNS, application callbacks, custom data sources, and provider code outside the supported I/O path cannot be forcibly terminated.
      Completion waits for that code and required cleanup to return. Observer work is excluded from the send budget, but the default inline
      observer can still delay completion. Deadline signalling itself never runs observers or CompletableFuture continuations.</p>
    <p>Simple batches stop at the first failed/cancelled/expired attempt. Untouched emails receive no callbacks; a batch cancelled or rejected
      before execution does not open its iterable. Validation, rehearsal, connection tests, and standalone BatchTransportExecutor operations
      are not instrumented by this Mailer deadline or cancellation API.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<a href="#section-batch-and-clustering" id="section-batch-and-clustering" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
<h2>Connection reuse and clusters</h2>
<section><p class="wide">Queue limits count waiting tasks; pool limits count reusable connections. A worker waiting for a lease is still active.
  Use the direct, simple-batch, open-connection, or pooled path that matches your workload.</p></section>
  <a href="#section-not-reusing-connections" id="section-not-reusing-connections" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  {{#> components/feature-heading-h3 core=true}}How direct connections work{{/components/feature-heading-h3}}

  <section>
	<div>
		<p class="wide">
			Direct asynchronous sending schedules send tasks on the mailer's configured <code class="inline language-java">ExecutorService</code>. Each send still opens and closes its own Transport
			connection, managed independently of a connection pool. By default this direct executor is a single-thread executor; provide a custom
			<code class="inline language-java">ExecutorService</code> if you want different concurrency. Every concurrent send still needs its own SMTP connection,
			which is less efficient in high-volume scenarios and can reach the SMTP server's connection limit more quickly.
		</p>
		<div>
			<pre><code class="language-java small">Mailer mailer = mailerBuilder.(..).buildMailer();

mailer.sync().sendMail(email); // blocks; failures are thrown here
CompletableFuture&lt;MailSubmissionReceipt&gt; future = mailer.async().sendMail(email).getCompletion();

// Runtime selection belongs in application code:
if (backgroundJob) {
    mailer.async().sendMail(email).getCompletion().whenComplete(this::recordResult);
} else {
    mailer.sync().sendMail(email);
}
</code></pre>
		</div><br/>
		Defining your own thread pool using an ExecutorService (the direct-sending default is
		<code class="inline language-java">Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor</code>):
		<div>
			<pre><code class="language-java small">mailerBuilder.withAsyncExecutionConfig(AsyncExecutionConfig.builder()
    .withExecutorService(new MyAwesomeCustomThreadPoolExecutor())
    .build())</code></pre>
		</div>
		<p class="wide">
			Queue, executor, pooling and timeout settings still come from the builder, properties or Spring. They configure resources, not which view an operation uses. If runtime data decides the mode, branch explicitly as above and handle asynchronous failures.
		</p>
		<p class="wide">
			See <a href="#section-mailer-lifecycle">Mailer lifecycle and resource ownership</a> for who shuts down the default and custom executors.
		</p>
	</div>
  </section>

  <section>
	<div class="view">
		<p>
			When reconnecting for every message is the only problem you need to solve, use simple sequential batch sending. It sends all provided emails
			over one SMTP connection. It consumes the iterable lazily, sends one at a time, stops at the first failure and reuses only that connection. Neither mode collects receipts: the sync call returns void, and the async completion carries Void. Use the configured MailSendResultObserver for per-email outcomes, including attempts before a later failure.
		</p>
		<p>Watch both the overall completion and the <a href="#section-mail-send-observer">terminal outcomes for reached emails</a>.
			Queue rejection, iteration, opening the connection or final shared-connection cleanup can fail outside an individual observed email.
			Untouched entries have no outcome. Observers retain their existing handoff, timing and delivery limits; lazy-batch factories run on the
			execution thread. Applications own business correlation, counts, persistence and retry decisions, and a count alone does not establish safe retry.
			Use <code>withOpenConnection(...)</code> when your application deliberately checkpoints after each returned receipt.</p>
	</div>

	<div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-java small">mailer.sync().sendMailsInSimpleBatch(emails); // blocks until the batch is done
MailSend&lt;Void&gt; batch = mailer.async().sendMailsInSimpleBatch(emails);
batch.getCompletion().join(); // optional: wait for the whole sequential batch
</code></pre>
		<p>
			For sustained high-volume sending, prefer <a href="#section-reusing-connections">pooled connections</a>.
		</p>
	</div>
  </section>

  <section>
	<div class="view">
		<p>
			When the caller owns the queue and needs custom work between successful sends, use
			<code class="inline language-java">withOpenConnection(...)</code>. Simple Java Mail owns and closes the SMTP connection for the callback, while the
			callback decides what to send next. This is useful for database-backed queues where a message should be marked as sent before the next one is read.
		</p>
		<p>Use the sender only on the thread running this callback, before the callback returns or throws. Don't pass it to another thread or save it
			for later: those calls are rejected before preparing or sending an email. Use <code>mailer.async().sendMail(...)</code> for independent
			asynchronous sends.</p>
		<p>
			This form uses the Mailer's built-in SMTP connection. It cannot be combined with a
			<a href="features.html#section-custom-mailer">CustomMailer</a>, which owns its own connection lifecycle. Use
			<code class="inline language-java">mailer.sync().sendMail(...)</code> or its async equivalent and keep any connection reuse inside that implementation.
		</p>
	</div>

	<div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-java small">mailer.withOpenConnection(sender -> {
    while (database.hasPendingMail()) {
        Email next = database.nextPendingMail();
        sender.sendMail(next);
        database.markSent(next);
    }
});
</code></pre>
		<p>
			The scoped sender is sequential, not asynchronous, not pooled and not clustered. For asynchronous queueing, higher throughput, pooled connections
			or clustered load balancing, use <a href="#section-reusing-connections">pooled connections</a>.
		</p>
		<p>
			Keep the receipt returned by <code class="inline language-java">sender.sendMail(next)</code> when the checkpoint needs provider-neutral submission
			status, accepted or unsent recipient groups, and any SMTP response exposed by the provider. You can ignore it when completion is enough. A failed or partial submission throws
			<code class="inline language-java">MailSubmissionException</code> with the same facts, so mark the queue item only after inspecting that outcome.
		</p>
	</div>
  </section>


  <a href="#section-reusing-connections" id="section-reusing-connections" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  {{#> components/feature-heading-h3 batch=true}}Reusing connections with a pool{{/components/feature-heading-h3}}

	<section>
		<div>
			<p class="wide">
				Pooled delivery reuses SMTP connections across worker threads. The default setup provides up to four pooled connections,
				which automatically close if inactive for five seconds following their last use. This mechanism is optimal for managing bursts of email traffic, keeping
				up to four connections active as long as needed.
			</p>
			<p class="wide">
				The connection pool configuration is flexible, allowing adjustments to core connection pool size, maximum pool size, and connection expiry policy.
			</p>
			<p class="wide">
				This section configures the Simple Java Mail <code>Mailer</code> path. Compare it with the standalone batch facade, direct leases, and
				provider-owned pooling in the <a href="smtp-connection-pooling.html">pool orchestration guide</a>.
			</p>
			<p>
				Direct sending can use a custom thread pool, while pooled sending also lets those threads reuse Transport connections. This can improve performance even with fewer
				threads, as it eliminates the need to repeatedly open and close connections for each email.
			</p>
		<div>
		<pre><code class="language-java small">Mailer pooledMailer = mailerBuilder
	   .(..)
	   .withConnectionPoolConfig(ConnectionPoolConfig.builder()
	       .withCoreSize(2) // keep 2 connections up at all times, automatically refreshed after expiry policy closes it (default 0)
	       .withMaxSize(10) // scale up to max 10 connections until expiry policy kicks in and cleans up (default 4)
	       .withClaimTimeout(Duration.ofMinutes(1)) // wait max 1 minute for available connection (default about 24.9 days)
	       .withExpireAfter(Duration.ofMinutes(30)) // keep connections spinning for half an hour (default 5 seconds)
	       .build())
	   .buildMailer();
	</code></pre>
				<p class="wide">
					Or using properties:
				</p>
				<pre><code class="language-properties small">simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.coresize=2
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.maxsize=10
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.claimtimeout.millis=60000
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.expireafter.millis=1800000
	</code></pre>
		  <p class="wide">
			  A live connection pool keeps the JVM running. See <a href="#section-mailer-lifecycle">Mailer lifecycle and resource ownership</a> for orderly
          application shutdown, including clustered Mailers.
		  </p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>


	<a href="#section-clustering" id="section-clustering" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
	{{#> components/feature-heading-h3 batch=true}}Clustered SMTP load balancing{{/components/feature-heading-h3}}

	<section>
		<div>
			<div class="view">
				<p>
					Use a cluster when several SMTP servers handle the same workload, or when different workloads need their own connection pools.
					Mailers that share a cluster key each register a pool for their configured SMTP server.
				</p>
				<p>
					For each send, the cluster selects one registered pool using its round-robin or random-access strategy.
					This spreads work across the SMTP servers registered for that cluster.
				</p>
				<p>
					For cluster-selected versus exact-Session callbacks outside the full Mailer API, see the
					<a href="smtp-connection-pooling.html#option-batch-facade">standalone batch-module facade</a>.
				</p>
			</div>

			<div class="side">
				Cluster-level Java config:
				<pre><code>UUID ordersCluster = UUID.fromString("00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000301");

Mailer clusteredMailer = mailerBuilder
    .(..) // normal SMTP settings
    .withConnectionPoolConfig(ConnectionPoolConfig.builder()
        .withClusterKey(ordersCluster)
        .withCoreSize(2)
        .withMaxSize(10)
        .withClaimTimeout(Duration.ofMinutes(1))
        .withExpireAfter(Duration.ofMinutes(30))
        .withLoadBalancingStrategy(LoadBalancingStrategy.ROUND_ROBIN)
        .build())
    .buildMailer();</code></pre>
				or using properties:
				<pre><code class="language-properties small">simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusterkey.uuid=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000301
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.loadbalancing.strategy=ROUND_ROBIN
# valid values: ROUND_ROBIN, RANDOM_ACCESS</code></pre>
			</div>

			<div class="wide full-width-example">
				<p><strong>Failure behavior in a cluster:</strong></p>
				<pre><code class="language-java small">SimpleJavaMail mail = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults();

Mailer primary = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer("smtp-primary.example.com", 587, "user", "password")
    .withConnectionPoolConfig(ConnectionPoolConfig.builder()
        .withClusterKey(myClusterKey)
        .build())
    .buildMailer();
Mailer secondary = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer("smtp-secondary.example.com", 587, "user", "password")
    .withConnectionPoolConfig(ConnectionPoolConfig.builder()
        .withClusterKey(myClusterKey)
        .build())
    .buildMailer();
Mailer tertiary = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer("smtp-tertiary.example.com", 587, "user", "password")
    .withConnectionPoolConfig(ConnectionPoolConfig.builder()
        .withClusterKey(myClusterKey)
        .build())
    .buildMailer();

try {
    primary.sync().sendMail(email); // one registered pool is selected for this send
} catch (MailException failure) {
    // The selected SMTP submission failed.
    // Its connection is discarded, but its server pool stays in the cluster.
    // Apply your retry, provider-routing, or circuit-breaking policy here.
}</code></pre>
			</div>
		</div>

		<p class="wide">
			<strong>Handling a failed send:</strong> Simple Java Mail invalidates the failed transport and reports the failure to your application.
			The server pool remains registered and participates in normal selection for later sends. With synchronous sending, the call throws
			<code class="inline language-java">MailException</code>; with asynchronous sending, the returned
			<a href="features.html#section-handling-async-result"><em>CompletableFuture</em> completes exceptionally</a>.
			Use that failure signal with your application's retry, health-check, circuit-breaking, or provider-routing policy, including its safeguards for
			ambiguous SMTP outcomes.
		</p>

	  <p class="wide">
		  <strong>Note 2:</strong> Connection pool defaults are scoped by cluster key. The first Mailer instance in a cluster sets that cluster's
		  pool defaults; later Mailer instances in the same cluster cannot change them. Different cluster keys can use different pool defaults.
	  </p>

	  <p class="wide">
		  <strong>Note 3:</strong> Using Java API, you can define any number of clusters. Using
		  <code class="inline language-properties">simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusterkey.uuid</code>, you define one default cluster key.
		  Using <code class="inline language-properties">simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.*</code>, property files and
		  <a href="/spring.html#section-spring-profiles">Spring</a> can also define
		  separate pool defaults for multiple cluster keys.
	  </p>

		<div class="wide">
			<p><strong>Property-defined cluster configs:</strong> Use one alias per cluster UUID. A named alias and a direct UUID alias may
              describe different clusters, but two aliases resolving to the same UUID are rejected during configuration loading.
              Keep all settings for that cluster under the chosen alias, including locked settings.</p>
			<pre><code class="language-properties small"># Alias-based config. The alias is only a property-file name; clusterkey.uuid is the actual cluster key.
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.orders.clusterkey.uuid=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000301
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.orders.coresize=0
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.orders.maxsize=3
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.orders.claimtimeout.millis=30000
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.orders.expireafter.millis=600000
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.orders.loadbalancing.strategy=RANDOM_ACCESS

# Direct UUID-keyed config. If clusterkey.uuid is omitted, the alias itself must be a UUID.
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000302.maxsize=8
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000302.loadbalancing.strategy=ROUND_ROBIN
</code></pre>
		</div>

		<p class="wide">Choose worker, queue, and connection limits together with your SMTP service's connection and rate limits.
          More connections are useful only while the server and your workload can use them.</p>
	</section>
  <a href="#section-mail-send-observer" id="section-mail-send-observer" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  {{#> components/feature-heading-h2 core=true}}Observing completed mail sends{{/components/feature-heading-h2}}

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        Configure one <code class="inline language-java">MailSendResultObserver</code> when audit or metrics code should see every completed email
        handled by a reusable Mailer. The builder keeps its usual <code class="inline language-java">with...</code> vocabulary; the
        <code class="inline language-java">onMailSendCompleted(...)</code> method is the callback Simple Java Mail invokes. Configuring another observer
        replaces the previous one.
      </p>
      <p>
        The observer receives one terminal <code class="inline language-java">MailSendOutcome</code> for each reached email. It covers the complete Simple
        Java Mail attempt, including preparation and asynchronous queue time. Its optional <code class="inline language-java">MailSubmissionReceipt</code>
        is narrower: it contains SMTP submission facts only. Preparation and scheduling failures therefore have no receipt; a
        <code class="inline language-java">MailSubmissionException</code> outcome contains that exception's exact receipt.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is a stream of completed-email progress, not a stream of queued, connecting, leased, or other transport lifecycle events. It does not expose
        the Email, rendered content, connection, pool, proxy, or executor.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-java small">Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer(
        "smtp.example.org", 587,
        "user", "password")
    .withSendHandlers(MailSendHandlers.builder().withResultObserver(
        this::recordMailSendOutcome).build())
    .buildMailer();

private void recordMailSendOutcome(
        MailSendOutcome outcome) {
    Duration totalTime = Duration.between(
        outcome.getRequestedAt(),
        outcome.getCompletedAt());

    audit.record(
        outcome.getEffectiveMessageId(),
        outcome.isSuccessful(),
        totalTime);

    outcome.getSubmissionReceipt()
        .ifPresent(receipt -> metrics
            .recordSubmissionStatus(
                receipt.getStatus()));

    outcome.getFailure()
        .ifPresent(failure -> metrics
            .recordFailure(failure.getClass()));
}</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
      <p>
        A named observer makes the configuration method and callback roles explicit:
      </p>
  <pre><code class="language-java small">final class MetricsMailSendObserver implements MailSendResultObserver {

    @Override
    public void onMailSendCompleted(MailSendOutcome outcome) {
        Instant startedAt = outcome.getStartedAt();
        Duration sendTime = startedAt == null ? null
            : Duration.between(startedAt, outcome.getCompletedAt());

        metrics.recordMailSend(
            outcome.isSuccessful(),
            outcome.isLoggingOnly(),
            sendTime);
    }
}

Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withSendHandlers(MailSendHandlers.builder().withResultObserver(new MetricsMailSendObserver()).build())
    .buildMailer();</code></pre>
      <p>
        <code class="inline language-java">requestedAt</code> records when the individual attempt begins,
        <code class="inline language-java">readyAt</code> when it is ready for execution admission,
        <code class="inline language-java">startedAt</code> when execution begins, before destination-specific preparation, and
        <code class="inline language-java">completedAt</code> when the terminal result exists. These wall-clock values are useful for correlation.
        For elapsed time, use <a href="/analyzing-send-results.html#section-send-timings"><code>outcome.getDiagnostics()</code></a>, collected automatically
        with the observer. Its monotonic scheduling measurement includes admission and worker waiting, not just time in a queue.
        A preparation failure reached during execution has ready and started times. A scheduling failure has a ready time but no started time.
        Unavailable ready/start values return <code>null</code>. The application metrics adapter above must treat a null duration as unmeasured,
        while still recording the outcome; it must not record it as a zero-duration sample.
      </p>
      <p>
        With the default registration, synchronous callbacks run before the call returns and async callbacks before the completion resolves.
        Executor-backed dispatch changes that boundary as described below. Outcomes are frozen before either callback execution or handoff.
      </p>
      <p>
        <code class="inline language-java">isLoggingOnly()</code> reports the Mailer's configured mode, not server acceptance. A successful logging-only
        attempt still has an <code class="inline language-java">UNKNOWN</code> submission receipt because no SMTP server was contacted. Message-ID getters
        return only real initial or effective Message-ID values; missing IDs remain <code class="inline language-java">null</code> rather than receiving a
        synthetic correlation value.
      </p>
      <p>
        The manually runnable
        <a href="https://github.com/bbottema/simple-java-mail/blob/codex/10.0.0/modules/simple-java-mail/src/test/java/demo/MailSendObserverDemoApp.java">MailSendObserverDemoApp</a>
        sends several emails asynchronously and prints each terminal outcome immediately with its Message-ID, individual measurements and total time, completion count,
        status, and callback thread. Enable <code class="inline language-java">DemoAppBase</code> logging-only mode to try the stream without an SMTP server.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>



<section>
  <div class="wide">
    <h3>Choose where the observer runs</h3>
    <table><thead><tr><th>Registration</th><th>Before send completion</th><th>Application responsibility</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td><code>withResultObserver(observer)</code></td><td>The callback returns before the synchronous call or async completion.</td>
        <td>Keep it quick and thread-safe; slow work delays that completion and occupies its notifying thread.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>withResultObserver(observer, executor)</code></td><td>SJM attempts one handoff before completing, without waiting for the callback.</td>
        <td>Own the executor, its queue, rejection policy, and shutdown. Track downstream completion yourself.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table>
    <p>Either overload replaces the whole registration; the one-argument overload restores inline dispatch.
      These methods belong to <code>MailSendHandlers.builder()</code>; install the immutable group with <code>withSendHandlers(...)</code>.
      These are runtime Java integrations, not properties or CLI values, and they are not exposed through OperationalConfig.</p>
    <p>Default callback threads are the calling thread for synchronous sends and preparation/admission failures, the send worker for running async
      work, and a private Mailer completion worker for cancelled/expired work that never started. Different sends can notify concurrently.
      A slow inline callback or completion continuation can delay other terminal notifications on that private worker, but not their deadline signals.</p>
    <p>The executor overload uses the application executor's actual policy. A direct executor or CallerRunsPolicy can still execute inline;
      a blocking execute() can delay handoff. Rejected handoffs are logged as undelivered notifications, without retry or inline fallback.
      A silent-discard executor cannot report a lost notification. Callback RuntimeExceptions are logged and ignored in both modes.</p>
    <p>Want updates before the result arrives? Use the separate <a href="/analyzing-send-results.html#section-live-progress">live-progress observer</a>.
      Its dispatcher keeps callbacks away from send threads, including when your executor runs tasks inline. Progress can be coalesced or dropped;
      the final result remains the source of truth.</p>
    <p>There are three distinct completions: the send and its cleanup, result-observer callback/handoff, and whatever downstream processing your observer
      starts. An observer may publish an event to a durable broker for night processing or separately scaled cloud workers. That downstream work
      need not limit SMTP throughput; its persistence, capacity and delivery guarantees belong to that application integration.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="wide full-width-example"><pre><code class="language-java">ExecutorService observationWorkers = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(2);
Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withSendHandlers(MailSendHandlers.builder().withResultObserver(outcome -&gt; auditQueue.publish(outcome), observationWorkers).build())
    .buildMailer();

// Sends may complete before publish() runs. A broker acknowledgement
// is separate again from processing that event on another system.
MailSend&lt;MailSubmissionReceipt&gt; send = mailer.async().sendMail(email);
send.getCompletion().join();

mailer.close();              // finishes SJM work and handoff attempts
observationWorkers.shutdown(); // application owns this executor
// Await its termination if this application needs all callbacks to finish.
// Await downstream acknowledgements separately if your workflow needs them.</code></pre>
    <p>The manual <a href="https://github.com/bbottema/simple-java-mail/blob/codex/10.0.0/modules/simple-java-mail/src/test/java/demo/MailSendExecutionDemoApp.java">MailSendExecutionDemoApp</a>
      requests cancellation and uses an executor-backed observer. It awaits send completion and observer aggregation separately.</p>
  </div>
</section>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <h3>Choose handlers for each send</h3>
    <p>Use <code>withSendHandlersFactory(sendId -&gt; ...)</code> when each send needs its own application context or callbacks.
      Simple Java Mail generates one UUID and calls the factory once per logical email, before preparation and individual-send admission.
      Retries and failover retain that identity and those handlers. Sending the same Email twice creates two separate identities.</p>
    <pre><code class="language-java">mailerBuilder.withSendHandlersFactory(sendId -&gt; MailSendHandlers.builder()
    .withProgressObserver(progress -&gt; dashboard.update(sendId, progress))
    .withResultObserver(outcome -&gt; audit.record(sendId, outcome))
    .build());</code></pre>
    <p>Fixed handlers and factory-produced handlers both receive notifications, fixed first, with callback failures isolated independently.
      For a single send the factory runs on the caller thread; lazy batches invoke it only for each reached Email on the executing thread.
      Rehearsals and connection tests do not invoke it. A factory exception or null result stops that email before submission; fixed handlers
      and enabled telemetry can still report a factory failure once an ID exists.</p>
    <p><code>withSendIdGenerator(...)</code> replaces the UUID generator when your application owns send identities. It must be thread-safe and
      return unique, non-null IDs. Generation failure stops the send without an observation ID. Keep the default when no custom identity is needed.</p>
    <p>Use a group's <code>toBuilder()</code> to edit result or progress registration independently, then supply the rebuilt group.
      <code>clearSendHandlers()</code> removes fixed registrations; <code>clearSendHandlersFactory()</code> removes the per-send factory;
      <code>resetSendIdGenerator()</code> restores random UUIDs. Existing Mailers and borrowed executors remain unchanged.</p>
  </div></section>
  <a href="#section-correlate-application-jobs" id="section-correlate-application-jobs" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>Correlate sends with application jobs</h3>
  <section>
    <div class="wide">
      <p>One shared Mailer can send invoice-1001 and invoice-1002 while keeping each job's progress and full outcome together,
        even when both calls reuse the same Email. Supply a handler factory that captures the current job ID on the submitting thread.
        Its callbacks retain that value after the submission call returns.</p>
      <p>The application-owned
        <a href="/assets/guides/job-correlation/JobCorrelatedMailer.java">JobCorrelatedMailer helper</a>
        wraps individual asynchronous sends. It sets a private ThreadLocal only around <code>mailer.async().sendMail(email)</code>.
        Inside the factory it copies the job ID into a final local value; the progress and outcome closures use that capture.
        A <code>finally</code> block restores any previous value, or removes the entry, immediately after initiating the send.
        The callbacks never read the ThreadLocal later.</p>
      <p>These three identities have different jobs:</p>
      <table><thead><tr><th>Identity</th><th>Example</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        <tr><td>Application job ID</td><td><code>invoice-1001</code></td><td>Your business record, captured by the application helper.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Observation UUID</td><td>A separate generated UUID for each call</td><td>Correlates this logical send's progress, diagnostics and terminal outcome. Retries and failover retain it and the same captured handlers.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>MIME Message-ID</td><td><code>&lt;invoice-notice@example.org&gt;</code></td><td>Message content metadata. The reused Email below deliberately has the same Message-ID in both sends.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table>
      <p>The <a href="/assets/guides/job-correlation/JobCorrelationExample.java">complete example with imports</a>
        creates its callbacks and application-owned observer executor explicitly. Its method accepts an already approved SMTP builder;
        it runs only when your application calls it. Both submissions use the same helper and its shared Mailer:</p>
<pre><code class="language-java small">public static void sendTwoInvoices(SimpleJavaMail mail, MailerRegularBuilder&lt;?&gt; approvedSmtpBuilder)
        throws Exception {
    Email sameEmail = mail.emailBuilder().startingBlank()
            .from("invoices@example.org")
            .to("customer@example.org")
            .withSubject("Your invoice is available")
            .withPlainText("Sign in to view your invoices.")
            .fixingMessageId("&lt;invoice-notice@example.org&gt;")
            .buildEmail();

    ExecutorService observationWorkers = Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor();
    JobCorrelatedMailer.JobEvents events = new JobCorrelatedMailer.JobEvents() {
        @Override
        public void onProgress(String jobId, UUID observationId, MailSendProgress progress) {
            System.out.printf("%s / %s: %s%n", jobId, observationId, progress.getStage());
        }

        @Override
        public void onOutcome(String jobId, UUID observationId, MailSendOutcome outcome) {
            System.out.printf("%s / %s: successful=%s%n", jobId, observationId, outcome.isSuccessful());
        }
    };

    try (JobCorrelatedMailer jobs = new JobCorrelatedMailer(approvedSmtpBuilder, events, observationWorkers)) {
        MailSend&lt;MailSubmissionReceipt&gt; first = jobs.sendAsync("invoice-1001", sameEmail);
        MailSend&lt;MailSubmissionReceipt&gt; second = jobs.sendAsync("invoice-1002", sameEmail);
        first.getCompletion().join();
        second.getCompletion().join();
    } finally {
        observationWorkers.shutdown();
        if (!observationWorkers.awaitTermination(30, TimeUnit.SECONDS)) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Outcome callbacks have not drained");
        }
    }
}</code></pre>
      <p>Individual async sends invoke the handler factory on their caller thread, so the helper captures each job before that scope ends.
        Lazy batches initialize their per-email handlers on the execution thread; a caller's ThreadLocal does not automatically travel there.
        Use this wrapper for individual sends only. The application must choose a separate propagation approach for a lazy batch.</p>
      <p>Progress is lossy: updates can be combined or dropped and can arrive after the terminal outcome.
        Use the full outcome and any receipt to decide what happened, and do not require every progress stage to arrive.
        Reusing a business job ID in another call still creates another observation UUID.</p>
      <p>The executor belongs to the application. Closing the helper drains its Mailer's send work and observer handoff attempts;
        it does not drain executor-backed callbacks or make their storage durable. This example waits for its callback executor separately.
        Durable job/result storage, rejection handling and downstream acknowledgements remain application responsibilities.</p>
      <p>The accompanying <a href="/assets/guides/job-correlation/README.md">integration and verification notes</a> explain the helper's scope.
        Its executable fixture uses a CustomMailer without SMTP and checks two concurrent sends, two queued sends, a capacity rejection,
        captured job/outcome identities and immediate context cleanup. It deliberately makes no promise about receiving every progress event.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <a href="#section-mailer-lifecycle" id="section-mailer-lifecycle" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  {{#> components/feature-heading-h2 batch=true spring=true}}Mailer lifecycle and resource ownership{{/components/feature-heading-h2}}

  <section>
    <div>
      <p class="wide">
        A <code class="inline language-java">Mailer</code> is designed to be built once and reused. When your application is finished with it, call
        <code class="inline language-java">mailer.close()</code> directly or let a try-with-resources block call it. Which resources need attention depends
        on how the Mailer is used and whether its executor came from Simple Java Mail or from your application.
      </p>
      <p class="wide">
        Closing a Mailer stops new work on its built-in executor, waits for accepted tasks to finish, and then closes its connection-pool registration.
        It also tracks sends accepted through a caller-owned executor and its private completion worker, without shutting down the caller's executor.
        It waits for result-observer handoff attempts, not executor-backed callbacks or external downstream processing.
        Live-progress reporting is separate: shutdown discards pending updates after sending drains, without waiting for or interrupting progress callbacks.
      </p>
      <p class="wide">
        Once shutdown starts, connection tests and probes cannot open another connection. An asynchronous connection test checks again when its
        task starts, in case the Mailer closed while it waited. Build a new Mailer if you need to reconnect after closing this one.
      </p>
      <p class="wide">
        Enabled write timeouts also need a background monitoring thread. Connections from each Angus Session created by Simple Java Mail share that thread;
        it stops when the last connection closes, not when a reusable connection goes back to the pool. You do not need to manage it yourself.
        If you supplied your own timeout scheduler, it remains yours to close. See the
        <a href="configuration.html#section-config-mailer">socket-timeout configuration</a> for the details.
      </p>

      <table>
        <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>How the Mailer is used</th>
          <th>What it owns</th>
          <th>What to do at shutdown</th>
        </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Synchronous direct sending</td>
          <td>Each SMTP connection is closed after the operation. The internal executor starts no worker thread unless an asynchronous operation is scheduled.</td>
          <td>Call <code class="inline language-java">mailer.close()</code> when its application lifetime ends. There are no send futures to await if it was only used synchronously.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Asynchronous sending with the default executor</td>
          <td>The Mailer owns its executor. Direct sending uses a single-thread executor by default.</td>
          <td>Call <code class="inline language-java">mailer.close()</code> to drain accepted work. Still inspect each returned future for send failures.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Sending with a custom <code class="inline language-java">ExecutorService</code></td>
          <td>The Mailer owns its SMTP resources; your application owns the executor it supplied.</td>
          <td>Wait for the futures and call <code class="inline language-java">mailer.close()</code>. Shut down the executor separately when its owner is finished with it.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Pooled sending</td>
          <td>The Mailer registers its SMTP connection pool and owns its default worker executor.</td>
          <td>Close every Mailer; its accepted sends drain before its pool registration closes. In a cluster, each Mailer has its own pool registration.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>A Spring-managed Mailer</td>
          <td>The same resources as its underlying configuration.</td>
          <td>Spring calls <code class="inline language-java">close()</code> on the managed Mailer when the application context stops. A Mailer created outside Spring bean management remains your responsibility.</td>
        </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p class="wide">
        Use <code class="inline language-java">close()</code> for normal application shutdown. It shuts down an executor created by Simple Java Mail and,
        when pooled delivery is active, closes the connection pool registered for that Mailer. It never shuts down an executor supplied through
        <code class="inline language-java">withExecutorService(...)</code>.
      </p>
      <p class="wide">Blocking <code>close()</code> declares <code>ExecutionException</code> for a cleanup failure and
        <code>InterruptedException</code> when the calling thread stops waiting. The original cleanup failure is the execution exception's cause.
        An interrupted wait does not cancel shutdown or prove cleanup finished; you can still observe that cleanup through <code>closeAsync()</code>.
        Code owning a Mailer's lifetime can declare just those two checked exceptions:</p>
<pre><code class="language-java small" id="example-mailer-close-exceptions">void runMailWork(MailerRegularBuilder&lt;?&gt; configuredBuilder, Iterable&lt;Email&gt; emails)
        throws ExecutionException, InterruptedException {
    try (Mailer mailer = configuredBuilder.buildMailer()) {
        for (Email email : emails) {
            mailer.sync().sendMail(email);
        }
    }
}</code></pre>
      <p class="wide">
        Use <code class="inline language-java">closeAsync()</code> to start the same cleanup without waiting in that call. Its
        <code class="inline language-java">CompletableFuture&lt;Void&gt;</code> completes after accepted work and owned resources have finished cleanup,
        including when there is no connection pool. Repeated calls join the same cleanup operation through independent completion views.
        Completing or cancelling one of those views does not stop cleanup or affect another caller.
        A successful cleanup does not mean that every send succeeded; those results remain on the individual send futures.
      </p>
      <p class="wide">
        Graceful shutdown does not request cancellation or add a deadline; configured send deadlines still apply. It can wait for slow I/O or an application callback. Call blocking
        <code class="inline language-java">close()</code> from the application's owning thread, not within a send operation or inline observer, which would wait for itself.
        You may start <code class="inline language-java">closeAsync()</code> there, but do not wait for its future from that same send or observer.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <h4>Synchronous work</h4>
      <p>
        A try-with-resources block can close the Mailer as soon as the final synchronous call returns.
      </p>
<pre><code class="language-java small">try (Mailer mailer = mailerBuilder
        .buildMailer()) {
    mailer.sync().sendMail(email);
}</code></pre>
    </div>

    <div class="side">
      <h4>Asynchronous work</h4>
      <p>
        With the built-in executor, try-with-resources drains accepted tasks when leaving the block. Await the futures as well if the caller needs their results.
      </p>
<pre><code class="language-java small">try (Mailer mailer = mailerBuilder.buildMailer()) {
    CompletableFuture&lt;MailSubmissionReceipt&gt; first =
            mailer.async().sendMail(firstEmail).getCompletion();
    CompletableFuture&lt;MailSubmissionReceipt&gt; second =
            mailer.async().sendMail(secondEmail).getCompletion();

    CompletableFuture.allOf(first, second)
            .join();
}</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-mail-send-retries" id="section-mail-send-retries" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Retry a temporary refusal without holding a worker</h2>
  <section><p class="wide">Optional <a href="/mail-send-retries.html">mail-send retries</a> keep one completion and one final observer outcome.
    Ordinary asynchronous retry waits release their worker, lease and proxy, then resume on the configured executor. Each actual invocation consumes
    the selected server's sending allowance. A partial or uncertain result is never automatically replayed. Simple batches and open-connection callbacks
    deliberately retain their connection and thread during waits; graceful shutdown drains accepted retries before closing owned execution resources.</p></section>
